    <!-- Disposal advice (until Llama answers come from the Pi) -->
    <string name="advice_cardboard">Flatten boxes and remove tape and polystyrene. Keep it dry — wet or greasy cardboard can\'t be recycled.</string>
    <string name="advice_paper">Keep it clean and dry. Shredded paper, tissues and greasy paper usually aren\'t accepted.</string>
    <string name="advice_plastic">Empty and rinse bottles, tubs and trays. Soft plastic film often needs a supermarket drop-off.</string>
    <string name="advice_metal">Rinse cans and tins. Scrunch clean foil into a ball. Empty aerosols are usually accepted.</string>
    <string name="advice_glass">Rinse bottles and jars and take the lids off. No drinking glasses, Pyrex or window glass.</string>
    <string name="advice_food_organics">Scrape it into your food caddy. Line it with a compostable bag or newspaper — never plastic.</string>
    <string name="advice_vegetation">Use your garden waste bin if you have one. No soil, stones, plastic pots or food.</string>
    <string name="advice_textile_trash">Not for household recycling. Donate wearable items, or take worn-out fabric to a textile bank.</string>
    <string name="advice_misc_trash">This can\'t be recycled at home. Bag it and put it in your general waste bin.</string>

